:"whitespace-pre-wrap text-sm font-medium leading-relaxed text-foreground"},"A nurse is preparing to administer digoxin 0.25 mg PO to an adult client diagnosed with heart failure. Which clinical assessment is a priority before administering this medication?"],["UL",{"class":"space-y-2"},["LI",{"class":"rounded-lg border border-border p-3 text-sm text-foreground-secondary"},["SPAN",{"class":"font-medium text-foreground"},"Measure the client's blood pressure in both arms"],["SPAN",{"class":"ml-2 text-xs"}],["P",{"class":"mt-1 text-xs"},"Blood pressure is monitored but apical heart rate is the decisive withholding parameter."]],["LI",{"class":"rounded-lg border border-border p-3 text-sm text-foreground-secondary"},["SPAN",{"class":"font-medium text-foreground"},"Auscultate the apical pulse rate for one full minute"],["SPAN",{"class":"ml-2 text-xs"},"Correct answer"," · Your selection"],["P",{"class":"mt-1 text-xs"},"Correct: Digoxin must be withheld if the apical heart rate is below 60 bpm in adults."]],["LI",{"class":"rounded-lg border border-border p-3 text-sm text-foreground-secondary"},["SPAN",{"class":"font-medium text-foreground"},"Check the client's capillary blood glucose level"],["SPAN",{"class":"ml-2 text-xs"}],["P",{"class":"mt-1 text-xs"},"Digoxin does not directly influence acute blood glucose."]],["LI",{"class":"rounded-lg border border-border p-3 text-sm text-foreground-secondary"},["SPAN",{"class":"font-medium text-foreground"},"Check the client's most recent sodium laboratory value"],["SPAN",{"class":"ml-2 text-xs"}],["P",{"class":"mt-1 text-xs"},"Potassium (hypokalemia), not sodium, potentiates digoxin toxicity."]]],["P",{"class":"border-t border-border pt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground-secondary"},"Digoxin is a cardiac glycoside with positive inotropic and negative chronotropic effects. The apical pulse must be counted for a full 60 seconds before administration. If the apical rate is less than 60 beats/min in an adult (or <90 in infants), the dose must be withheld and the healthcare provider notified to prevent fatal bradyarrhythmias."]],["DIV",{"class":"rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface text-foreground shadow-sm transition-all dark:border-border dark:bg-surface space-y-4 p-5"},["DIV",{"class":"flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-2"},["P",{"class":"text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-foreground-muted"},"Question ","2"],["SPAN",{"class":"text-xs font-semibold text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-300"},"Correct"]],["P",{"class":"whitespace-pre-wrap text-sm font-medium leading-relaxed text-foreground"},"A nurse is educating a client who is newly prescribed lithium carbonate for bipolar I disorder. Which clinical signs should the nurse teach the client to identify as early manifestations of lithium toxicity? (Select all that apply.)"],["UL",{"class":"space-y-2"},["LI",{"class":"rounded-lg border border-border p-3 text-sm text-foreground-secondary"},["SPAN",{"class":"font-medium text-foreground"},"Nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea"],["SPAN",{"class":"ml-2 text-xs"},"Correct answer"," · Your selection"],["P",{"class":"mt-1 text-xs"},"Early gastrointestinal toxicity cue."]],["LI",{"class":"rounded-lg border border-border p-3 text-sm text-foreground-secondary"},["SPAN",{"class":"font-medium text-foreground"},"Fine hand tremors becoming coarse"],["SPAN",{"class":"ml-2 text-xs"},"Correct answer"," · Your selection"],["P",{"class":"mt-1 text-xs"},"Early neuromuscular cue of toxic accumulation."]],["LI",{"class":"rounded-lg border border-border p-3 text-sm text-foreground-secondary"},["SPAN",{"class":"font-medium text-foreground"},"Constipation and urinary retention"],["SPAN",{"class":"ml-2 text-xs"}],["P",{"class":"mt-1 text-xs"},"Lithium typically causes polyuria and loose stools, not retention."]],["LI",{"class":"rounded-lg border border-border p-3 text-sm text-foreground-secondary"},["SPAN",{"class":"font-medium text-foreground"},"Slurred speech and muscle weakness"],["SPAN",{"class":"ml-2 text-xs"},"Correct answer"," · Your selection"],["P",{"class":"mt-1 text-xs"},"Early central nervous system impairment cue."]],["LI",{"class":"rounded-lg border border-border p-3 text-sm text-foreground-secondary"},["SPAN",{"class":"font-medium text-foreground"},"Hypertensive crisis and cardiac arrest"],["SPAN",{"class":"ml-2 text-xs"}],["P",{"class":"mt-1 text-xs"},"Hypertensive crisis is characteristic of MAOI interactions, not early lithium toxicity."]]],["P",{"class":"border-t border-border pt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-foreground-secondary"},"Lithium has a narrow therapeutic window (0.6 to 1.2 mEq/L). Early manifestations of toxicity (serum level 1.5 to 2.0 mEq/L) include gastrointestinal disturbances (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea), neurological symptoms (fine hand tremors, muscle weakness, lethargy), and slurred speech. Polyuria and mild thirst are common side effects, but gross ataxia and seizures occur in severe toxicity (>2.5 mEq/L)."]]]]]]]]],"viewport":{"width":1280,"height":720},"timestamp":94262.551,"wallTime":1794377688735,"collectionTime":9.299999997019768,"resourceOverrides":[],"isMainFrame":true}}
